"1. Expert Rev Anti Infect Ther. 2026 Jun;24(6):545-586. doi: \n10.1080/14787210.2025.2477198. Epub 2025 Apr 15.\n\nCurrent access, availability and use of antibiotics in primary care among key \nlow- and middle-income countries and the policy implications.\n\nSaleem Z(1), Mekonnen BA(2), Orubu ES(3)(4), Islam MA(5)(6), Nguyen TTP(7), \nUbaka CM(8), Buma D(9), Thuy NDT(10), Sant Y(11), Sono TM(12)(13), Bochenek \nT(14), Kalungia AC(15), Abdullah S(1), Miljkovi\u0107 N(16), Yeika E(17), Niba \nLL(18)(19), Akafity G(20), Sefah IA(21), Opanga SA(22), Kitutu FE(23)(24)(25), \nKhuluza F(26), Zaranyika T(27), Parajuli A(28)(29), Darweesh O(30), Islam \nS(31)(32), Kumar S(33), Nabayiga H(34), Jairoun AA(35), Chigome A(12), Ogunleye \nO(36)(37), Fadare J(38)(39), Massele A(40), Cook A(41)(42), Jeli\u0107 AG(43), God\u00f3i \nIPD(44)(45), Phillip A(40), Meyer JC(12)(46), Funiciello E(41), Lorenzetti \nG(41), Kurdi A(12)(30)(47)(48), Haseeb A(49), Moore CE(41), Campbell SM(12)(50), \nGodman B(12)(41)(48)(51), Sharland M(41).\n\nAuthor information:\n(1)Department of Pharmacy Practice, Faculty of Pharmacy, Bahauddin Zakariya \nUniversity, Multan, Pakistan.\n(2)Department of Pharmacy, College of Medicine and Health Sciences, Bahir Dar \nUniversity, Bahir Dar, Ethiopia.\n(3)Niger Delta University, Wilberforce Island, Bayelsa State, Nigeria.\n(4)Institute for Health System Innovation & Policy, Boston University, Boston, \nMA, USA.\n(5)Infectious Diseases Division, International Centre for Diarrhoeal Disease \nResearch, Dhaka, Bangladesh.\n(6)School of Population Health, Faculty of Medicine and Health, University of \nNew South Wales, Sydney, Australia.\n(7)Pharmaceutical Administration PharmacoEconomics, Hanoi University of \nPharmacy, Hanoi, Vietnam.\n(8)Public Health Supply Chain and Pharmacy Practice Research Unit, Department of \nClinical Pharmacy and Pharmacy Management, Faculty of Pharmaceutical Sciences, \nFaculty of Pharmaceutical Sciences, University of Nigeria Nsukka Campus, Nsukka, \nNigeria.\n(9)Muhimbili National Hospital, Dar Es Salaam, Tanzania.\n(10)Oxford Clinical Research Unit, Hanoi, Vietnam.\n(11)Department of Pharmacology, Karnavati School of Dentistry, Ahmedabad, India.\n(12)Department of Public Health Pharmacy and Management, School of Pharmacy, \nSefako Makgatho Health Sciences University, Garankuwa, South Africa.\n(13)Saselamani Pharmacy, Saselamani, South Africa.\n(14)Institute of Public Health, Faculty of Health Sciences, Jagiellonian \nUniversity Medical College, Krakow, Poland.\n(15)Department of Pharmacy, School of Health Sciences, University of Zambia, \nLusaka, Zambia.\n(16)Hospital Pharmacy, Institute of Orthopaedics Banjica, Belgrade, Serbia.\n(17)Programs coordinator/Technical Supervisor for HIV/Malaria, Delegation of \nPublic Health, North West Region, Cameroon.\n(18)Department of Public Health, The University of Bamenda, Bamenda, Cameroon.\n(19)Effective Basic Services Africa, Bamenda, Africa.\n(20)Research Unit, Cape Coast Teaching Hospital, Cape Coast, Ghana.\n(21)Pharmacy Practice Department, School of Pharmacy, University of Health and \nAllied Sciences, Volta Region, Ghana.\n(22)Department of Pharmacology, Clinical Pharmacy and Pharmacy Practice, School \nof Pharmacy, University of Nairobi, Nairobi.\n(23)Sustainable Pharmaceutical Systems (SPS) Unit, School of Health Sciences, \nMakerere University, Kampala, Uganda.\n(24)Department of Women's and Children's Health, International Maternal and \nChild Health (IMCH), Uppsala University, Uppsala, Sweden.\n(25)Department of Pharmacy, Makerere University School of Health Sciences, \nKampala, Uganda.\n(26)Pharmacy Department, Formerly College of Medicine, Kamuzu University of \nHealth Sciences (KUHeS), Blantyre, Malawi.\n(27)Department of Medicine, University of Zimbabwe College of Health Sciences, \nHarare, Zimbabwe.\n(28)HERD International, Lalitpur, Nepal.\n(29)Public Health Research Society, Kathmandu, Nepal.\n(30)Department of Clinical Laboratory Sciences, College of Pharmacy, Al-Kitab \nUniversity, Kirkuk, Iraq.\n(31)Department of Microbiology, Jahangirnagar University, Dhaka, Bangladesh.\n(32)School of Optometry and Vision Science, UNSW Sydney, New South Wales, \nAustralia.\n(33)Department of Periodontology and Implantology, Karnavati School of \nDentistry, Karnavati University, Gandhinagar, India.\n(34)Management Science Department, Strathclyde Business School, University of \nStrathclyde, Glasgow, UK.\n(35)Health and Safety Department, Dubai Municipality, Dubai, United Arab \nEmirates.\n(36)Department of Pharmacology, Therapeutics and Toxicology, Lagos State \nUniversity College of Medicine Ikeja, Nigeria.\n(37)Department of Medicine, Lagos State University Teaching Hospital, Ikeja, \nNigeria.\n(38)Department of Pharmacology and Therapeutics, Ekiti State University, Ado, \nNigeria.\n(39)Department of Medicine, Ekiti State University Teaching Hospital, Ado, \nNigeria.\n(40)Department of Clinical Pharmacology and Therapeutics, School of Medicine \nKairuki University, Tanzania.\n(41)Centre for Neonatal and Paediatric Infection, Institute for Infection and \nImmunity, City St. George's, University of London, London, UK.\n(42)Health Economics Research Centre, Nuffield Department of Population Health, \nUniversity of Oxford, Oxford, UK.\n(43)Department of Pharmacy, Faculty of Medicine, University of Banja Luka, Banja \nLuka, Republic of Srpska, Bosnia & Herzegovina.\n(44)Institute of Pharmaceutical Sciences, Federal University of Rio de Janeiro, \nRio de Janeiro, Brazil.\n(45)Health Technology Assessment Center - Management, Economics, Health \nEducation and Pharmaceutical Services, Federal University of Rio de Janeiro, \nBrazil.\n(46)South African Vaccination and Immunisation Centre, Sefako Makgatho Health \nSciences University, Garankuwa, South Africa.\n(47)Department of Clinical Pharmacy, College of Pharmacy, Hawler Medical \nUniversity, Erbil, Iraq.\n(48)Strathclyde Institute of Pharmacy and Biomedical Science, University of \nStrathclyde, Glasgow, UK.\n(49)Clinical Pharmacy Department, Al Rayan National College of Health Sciences \nand Nursing, Al-Madinah Al-Munawarah, Saudi Arabia.\n(50)School of Health Sciences, University of Manchester, Manchester, UK.\n(51)Centre of Medical and Bio-Allied Health Sciences Research, Ajman University, \nAjman, United Arab Emirates.\n\nINTRODUCTION: Antimicrobial resistance (AMR) poses a significant threat, \nparticularly in low- and middle-income countries (LMICs), exacerbated by \ninappropriate antibiotic use, access to quality antibiotics and weak \nantimicrobial stewardship (AMS). There is a need to review current evidence on \nantibiotic use, access, and AMR, in primary care across key countries.\nAREAS COVERED: This narrative review analyzes publications from 2018 to 2024 \nregarding access, availability, and use of appropriate antibiotics.\nEXPERT OPINION: There were very few studies focussing on a lack of access to \nantibiotics in primary care. However, there was considerable evidence of high \nrates of inappropriate antibiotic use, including Watch antibiotics, typically \nfor minor infections, across studied countries exacerbated by patient demand. \nThe high costs of antibiotics in a number of LMICs impact on their use, \nresulting in short courses and sharing of antibiotics. This can contribute to \nAMR alongside the use of substandard and falsified antibiotics. Overall, limited \nimplementation of national action plans, insufficient resources, and knowledge \ngaps affects sustainable development goals to provide routine access to safe, \neffective, and appropriate antibiotics.\nCONCLUSIONS: There is a clear need to focus health policy on the optimal use of \nessential AWaRe antibiotics in primary care settings to reduce AMR in LMICs.\n\nDOI: 10.1080/14787210.2025.2477198\nPMID: 40110804 [Indexed for MEDLINE]"